'''''Curcuma''''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|ɜːr|k|j|ʊ|m|ə}})<ref>{{Dictionary.com|curcuma}}</ref> is a [[genus]] of plants in the ginger family [[Zingiberaceae]] that contains such species as [[turmeric]] and [[Siam tulip]]. They are native to Southeast Asia, southern China, the Indian Subcontinent, New Guinea and northern Australia.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Curcuma [family ZINGIBERACEAE] |journal=Global Plants |url=https://plants.jstor.org/stable/10.5555/al.ap.flora.fna022_curcuma }}</ref> Some species are reportedly naturalized in other warm parts of the world such as tropical Africa, Central America, Florida, and various islands of the Pacific, Indian and Atlantic Oceans. Generally, most curcuma grows well in loose and sandy soil in shaded areas.{{R|POWO}}<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sirirugsa |first1=P. |last2=Larsen |first2=K. |last3=Maknoi |first3=C. |title=The Genus ''Curcuma'' L. (Zingiberaceae): Distribution and Classification with Reference to Species Diversity in Thailand |s2cid=173169715 |url=https://www.nparks.gov.sg/sbg/research/publications/gardens-bulletin-singapore/-/media/sbg/gardens-bulletin/4-4-59-1-2-19-y2007-v59-p1-p2-gbs-pg-203.pdf |journal=Gardens' Bulletin Singapore |volume=59 |issue=1&2 |pages=203–220 |year=2007 |access-date=2021-10-20 |archive-date=2021-07-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210725163322/https://www.nparks.gov.sg/sbg/research/publications/gardens-bulletin-singapore/-/media/sbg/gardens-bulletin/4-4-59-1-2-19-y2007-v59-p1-p2-gbs-pg-203.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

== Botanical description ==
Curcuma is a [[Perennial plant|perennial]], [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] plant that can reach a height of {{Convert|1|m|sp=us}}. It emits numerous, edible [[Rhizome|rhizomes]] whose interiors are yellow or orange. These rhizomes are reduced to a powder, which is the spice called curcuma. Its [[lanceolate]] leaves are oblong or elliptical and are of a uniform green, and about {{Convert abbreviated|50|cm}} long and {{Convert abbreviated|7 to 25|cm}} wide.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Collectif|title=Histoire Naturelle|publisher=Flammarion|year=2016|isbn=9782081378599|location=Paris|pages=149|language=French}}</ref>  

==Uses==
The name is derived from the Sanskrit {{Lang|sa-Latn|kuṅkuma}}, referring to [[turmeric]]. Turmeric is used to flavour or colour curry powders, mustards, butters, and cheeses; it may also be used as a substitute for [[saffron]] or other yellowish pigments.<ref>{{cite book |translator-last= Tawney |translator-first= C. H. |editor-last= Penzer |editor-first= N. M. |year= 2014 |isbn= 978-935050135-1 |author= Somadeva |author-link= Somadeva |orig-year= 1070 & 1924 |chapter= Ch. 104 - The use of turmeric (kuṅkuma) in ancient India |volume= XIII |trans-title= The Ocean of Story |title= Kathasaritsagara |title-link= Kathasaritsagara |page= 13 |publisher= B.R. Publishing Corporation |chapter-url= https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/kathasaritsagara-the-ocean-of-story/d/doc363248.html }}</ref>

